<onlyinclude>'''Power electronics''' studies switched energy-conversion networks, including their steady-state conversion laws, transient control, semiconductor losses, magnetic design and electrical/thermal safety. A practical converter is a coupled electrical, control and heat-transfer system rather than an ideal switch network.</onlyinclude>

''Reading depth: [[Power electronics/Simple|Simple]] · [[Power electronics/Technical|Technical]].''

== Scope and converter families ==
Power electronics covers AC–DC rectification, DC–DC buck/boost conversion, DC–AC inversion, AC–AC conversion and transformer-isolated conversion. Active rectifiers can control power factor and permit bidirectional operation when designed for it. Power conversion differs from low-power signal conditioning in its energy throughput, but the distinction is functional rather than an absolute wattage threshold. Source/load impedance, thermal design, safety and electromagnetic compatibility constrain each implementation.

== Switched-converter model ==
Consider an ideal nonisolated buck converter in continuous conduction with constant input voltage V_in, duty fraction D (0 ≤ D ≤ 1), switching period T_s, output V_o and inductance L. During the on interval, the inductor voltage is V_in − V_o; during the off interval it is −V_o. Periodic steady state requires zero net inductor volt-seconds:
: D(V_in − V_o)T_s + (1 − D)(−V_o)T_s = 0.
Thus V_o = D V_in. The corresponding ideal inductor ripple (peak-to-peak) is
: ΔI_L = (V_in − V_o)D/(L f_s).
These equalities assume ideal switches, negligible losses and parasitics, sufficiently large output capacitance and continuous inductor current. They do not describe discontinuous conduction, startup or closed-loop stability.

== State-space interpretation and experimental boundaries ==
For the ideal buck with resistive load R and capacitor C, using state x = [i_L, v_o]ᵀ and switch position u ∈ {0,1}, the piecewise dynamics are L di_L/dt = u V_in − v_o and C dv_o/dt = i_L − v_o/R. Averaging over a fixed-frequency switching period yields L d⟨i_L⟩/dt ≈ d V_in − ⟨v_o⟩ and C d⟨v_o⟩/dt ≈ ⟨i_L⟩ − ⟨v_o⟩/R, assuming switching ripple is small relative to slow envelope dynamics. Linearization around an operating point is local: it does not validate startup, saturation, discontinuous conduction or nonlinear protection transitions. For a resistive load, continuous conduction requires the minimum inductor current to remain above zero; a useful first screening condition is I_out > ΔI_L/2.

== Loss accounting ==
A first-order MOSFET conduction estimate is P_cond = I_rms² R_DS(on), evaluated at the operating junction temperature. For approximately linear hard-switching voltage/current overlap, a rough transition-loss estimate is P_sw ≈ (V I/2)(t_r + t_f)f_s. This expression is a screening approximation, not a substitute for datasheet E_on/E_off curves or double-pulse characterization; capacitance energy, reverse recovery, dead-time diode conduction, magnetic and capacitor losses can be material. Gate-drive energy is often approximated by Q_g V_drive f_s, but is primarily dissipated in the drive path rather than necessarily in the switch die.

Overall efficiency is η = P_out/P_in = P_out/(P_out + P_loss). Heat must be removed within the actual thermal network. A simplified steady-state temperature estimate T_j ≈ T_a + P_device R_θJA assumes the quoted thermal resistance is applicable to the real PCB, airflow and mounting.

== Numerical consistency check ==
For an ideal 24 V to 12 V buck at 100 kHz with L = 100 μH, D = 0.5 and ΔI_L = (24 − 12)×0.5/(100×10⁻⁶×100000) = 0.6 A peak-to-peak. If delivered output power is 120 W and total losses are 10 W, efficiency is 120/130 ≈ 92.31%, with 10 W requiring thermal management. These are independent illustrative assumptions: the ideal voltage and ripple equations do not establish a real converter's 10 W loss budget.

== Topology, protection and constraints ==
A synchronous half-bridge needs nonoverlapping gate commands to avoid shoot-through; required dead time depends on worst-case driver and switch delays. Excess dead time increases diode conduction and can degrade waveforms. Parasitic inductance and rapid di/dt can create overvoltage; rapid dv/dt contributes to EMI and unintended coupling. Snubbers, layout, filtering and suitable gate drive must be assessed at the intended operating point. Galvanic isolation, creepage/clearance, overcurrent and overtemperature protection require application-specific standards and validation; a general article cannot certify a design.

MOSFETs are frequently attractive at high switching frequencies; IGBTs can be useful at higher powers and voltages. Silicon carbide and gallium nitride devices can enable faster switching, but introduce distinct gate-drive, packaging, reliability and EMI challenges. Selection must use device-specific ratings and measured behavior, not generic technology rankings.

== Verification and architecture research ==
Measure efficiency with simultaneous calibrated input/output voltage and current readings at specified load, temperature and switching frequency; report instrument bandwidth and uncertainty. Characterize switching transitions with voltage/current waveforms and a suitable probe layout; a double-pulse test can isolate device switching energy under specified current, voltage, gate drive and temperature. Measure conducted/radiated EMI and load-step response under a documented test setup rather than inferring them from switching frequency alone. The inductor's saturation current, copper/core losses, capacitor RMS ratings, control-loop stability and PCB parasitics bound the achievable power density. A promising research direction is co-optimizing converter switching, distributed on-package power delivery and workload-driven dynamic voltage scaling; performance benefits are hypotheses until demonstrated under controlled load profiles.

== Provenance and limitations ==
The buck derivation follows ideal periodic inductor volt-second balance; the numerical example is an original worked calculation. Semiconductor loss approximations follow the design framework in Texas Instruments' LM3753/LM3754 controller datasheet, which discusses conduction, switching and gate-drive contributions. Infineon's IGBT dead-time guidance motivates the half-bridge interlock discussion. Both vendor sources are application-specific and should not be interpreted as universal device measurements.

